      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=18(t+3)^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n18(t+3)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=-3.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(-3)=4\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=-3,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical.",
